"""The terms of the notebook that are defined where they first appear (the builder checks it) and the one-line definitions of Appendix B."""

GLOSSARY = {
    "drug": "a molecule that changes how something in the body works",
    "ADME": "absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion: the four stages of a drug's life in the body",
    "enzyme": "a protein that speeds up one particular chemical reaction and is not used up by it",
    "cytochrome P450": "a family of liver enzymes (CYP) that does most of the chemical modification of drugs, with a heme at the centre of each",
    "heme": "a flat ring-shaped molecule that holds one iron atom, at the centre of every P450 enzyme",
    "inhibit": "to reduce the activity of an enzyme",
    "drug-drug interaction": "a change in the effect of one drug because another drug alters how it is removed from the body",
    "reversible inhibition": "inhibition that lasts only while the inhibitor is present",
    "time-dependent inhibition": "inhibition that grows with the time enzyme and molecule spend together and outlasts the molecule",
    "TDI": "the abbreviation of time-dependent inhibition",
    "gate": "a pass/fail rule for an experiment, written down before its result exists",
    "Type I": "binding of a molecule near the heme in which the iron keeps its water molecule",
    "Type II": "binding in which a nitrogen atom of the molecule takes the place of the water on the iron",
    "SMARTS": "a text notation for chemical substructures, used to search molecules for a group of atoms",
    "Boltz-2": "a co-folding model that predicts the structure of a protein together with the molecules bound to it",
    "co-folding": "predicting the three-dimensional structure of a protein and the molecules bound to it together, from their sequences and structures",
    "cryo-EM": "an experimental technique that determines structures by imaging frozen molecules with an electron microscope",
    "ångström": "a unit of length of 0.1 nanometre; a bond between two atoms is about 1 to 2 ångström long",
    "blind": "a test whose correct answers are kept by the organisers and not shown to the participants",
    "Matthews correlation coefficient": "a score for yes/no predictions: 0 for guessing, 1 for perfect, honest when yes is rare",
    "MCC": "the abbreviation of the Matthews correlation coefficient",
    "LDDT-PLI": "a score from 0 to 1 for how well the distances between a ligand's atoms and the nearby protein atoms are reproduced",
    "PoseBusters": "a program that checks a predicted structure for physical and chemical sanity",
    "SMILES": "a line of text that spells out the atoms and bonds of a molecule",
    "pIC50": "the negative base-10 logarithm of the concentration (mol/L) that halves an enzyme's activity; larger means stronger",
    "fingerprint": "a list of yes/no answers to the question whether a molecule contains each of many small groups of atoms",
    "stacking": "a final step that learns how much to trust each of several models and blends their scores",
    "elimination rate constant": "the constant k in dC/dt = -kC: the fraction of the drug removed per unit time",
    "differential equation": "an equation that relates a quantity to its own rate of change",
    "half-life": "the time it takes a concentration to fall to half",
    "clearance": "the volume of blood completely cleared of a drug per unit time",
    "steady state": "the state in which the amount of drug (or enzyme) entering equals the amount leaving",
    "area under the concentration curve": "the integral of the concentration over time, a measure of exposure to a drug",
    "substrate": "the molecule an enzyme acts on",
    "Michaelis constant": "K_M, the substrate concentration at which an enzyme works at half its maximum rate",
    "Michaelis-Menten equation": "the rate of an enzyme as a function of substrate concentration, v = Vmax [S] / (KM + [S])",
    "intrinsic clearance": "Vmax / KM, the clearance of an enzyme at substrate concentrations far below KM",
    "monooxygenase": "an enzyme that inserts one oxygen atom of an oxygen molecule into its substrate",
    "axial cysteine": "the cysteine of the protein whose sulfur binds the heme iron from below the ring",
    "IC50": "the concentration of an inhibitor that halves the activity of an enzyme in a given assay",
    "mechanism-based inactivator": "a molecule that the enzyme converts, in its normal cycle, into a species that reacts with the enzyme and disables it",
    "observed inactivation rate": "k_obs = k_inact [I] / (K_I + [I]), the rate at which activity is lost at a given inhibitor concentration",
    "dose-response curve": "the activity of an enzyme as a function of the concentration of an inhibitor",
    "Hill slope": "the exponent h of the Hill equation, which sets how steeply the activity falls",
    "Hill equation": "y = 1 / (1 + ([I] / IC50)^h), the activity left at inhibitor concentration [I]",
    "shift": "s = t - d: the difference between the pIC50 after pre-incubation and the pIC50 without it",
    "coordinate bond": "a bond in which both electrons come from one of the two atoms",
    "lone pair": "a pair of electrons that an atom holds without sharing it in a bond",
    "structural alert": "a substructure known to be connected with the formation of reactive metabolites",
    "valence": "the number of bonds an atom of an element usually forms: 4 for carbon, 3 for nitrogen, 2 for oxygen, 1 for hydrogen",
    "molecular graph": "a molecule as a mathematical graph: the atoms are the vertices and the bonds are the edges",
    "adjacency matrix": "a square table with one row and column per atom, holding 1 where two atoms share a bond and 0 elsewhere",
    "aromatic": "of a ring: flat, with a p orbital on every atom and 4n + 2 pi electrons shared round it, which makes it unusually stable",
    "canonical SMILES": "the one SMILES string that a fixed rule picks among all the strings that describe a molecule",
    "enantiomer": "one of two mirror-image forms of a molecule that cannot be turned into each other without breaking bonds",
    "racemate": "an equal mixture of the two enantiomers of a molecule; a SMILES without stereo marks describes it",
    "inferred positive": "a compound labelled positive because it was inactive without pre-incubation (pIC50 below 4) and active with it (pIC50 above 4.301)",
    "standard deviation": "the typical size of the error of an estimate: the spread of the estimates if the experiment were repeated",
    "reactive notebook": "a notebook that knows which cell uses which variable and runs a cell again whenever something it uses changes, so that the page never shows a stale result",
    "heme frame": "a coordinate system fixed to the heme: the iron at the origin, one axis along the normal of the porphyrin plane, so that ligands of different crystal structures can be compared",
    "pharmacophore hypothesis": "a statement that a molecule must offer a given arrangement of chemical features (for example an acceptor, an aromatic ring and a hydrophobic group at stated distances) to bind",
    "log-likelihood ratio": "the natural logarithm of how much more probable an observation is under one class than under another; positive when it favours the first class",
    "chi-square statistic": "a number that measures how far a table of counts is from the table that would be expected if its rows and columns were unrelated; large when they are strongly related",
    "density field": "a number at every point of a grid in space, here the sum of Gaussian bumps centred on the positions of the features of many ligands, large where many ligands have such a feature",
    "standard error": "the typical size of the error of one estimate, computed from the data of that estimate (for a fitted curve, from how closely it follows the measurements); it predicts the spread the estimate would show if the experiment were repeated",
    "confidence interval": "a range around an estimate that holds the true value with a stated probability; for a normal error the 95 % interval is the estimate plus and minus 1.96 standard deviations",
    "gradient-boosted trees": "a model made of many small decision trees fitted one after another, each correcting the mistakes of those before it",
    "hit": "a compound that shows activity in a first screen and is therefore promoted to a full dose-response measurement",
    "selection bias": "the error that arises when the examples a model learns from were chosen by a process that is related to the label",
    "covariate shift": "a difference between two sets in the distribution of the structures, while the rule that links structure to label stays the same",
    "label shift": "a difference between two sets in the share of positives",
    "prevalence": "the proportion of positives in a set",
    "Tanimoto similarity": "the share of the fingerprint bits switched on in either of two molecules that are switched on in both",
    "density ratio": "p_test(x) / p_train(x), the factor by which a structure is more or less common in the test set than in the training set",
    "effective sample size": "the number of equally weighted compounds that carries the information of a weighted sample: (sum of the weights)^2 / sum of the squared weights",
    "accuracy": "the fraction of compounds called correctly",
    "precision": "of the compounds called positive, the fraction that are positive",
    "recall": "of the positive compounds, the fraction that were called positive",
    "loss function": "a number that says how bad a prediction is for a compound with a known label; training minimises its average",
    "log-loss": "minus the logarithm of the probability the model gave to the observed label, also called cross-entropy",
    "logit": "the score z of a model before it becomes a probability, p = 1 / (1 + exp(-z))",
    "likelihood": "the probability that a model assigns to all the observed labels together",
    "bias-variance trade-off": "more flexible models have less systematic error (bias) and more sensitivity to the particular training set (variance)",
    "decision tree": "a flowchart of yes/no questions about the features that ends in a prediction",
    "Gini impurity": "2p(1 - p) for a group with a fraction p of positives: zero for a pure group, largest at one half",
    "bootstrap sample": "n compounds drawn with replacement from n, which holds about 63 % of the distinct compounds",
    "kernel": "a symmetric, positive semi-definite similarity function K(x, x') that equals a dot product in some feature space",
    "activation function": "the non-linear function applied to the weighted sum of a neuron's inputs, such as tanh or the rectified linear unit",
    "backpropagation": "the chain rule applied from the output of a network backwards, to find the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight",
    "multitask": "of a network: one shared trunk and one head per task, trained on all tasks at once with a loss that skips missing labels",
    "message-passing neural network": "a network on a molecular graph in which every atom repeatedly combines messages from its bonded neighbours into its own state",
    "self-attention": "each token of a sequence takes a weighted average of the values of all tokens, with weights from the dot products of queries and keys",
    "transformer": "a network built from layers of self-attention and small per-token networks, that reads a sequence of tokens",
    "principal component analysis": "finding the directions along which a table varies most, as the eigenvectors of its covariance matrix",
    "average precision": "the area under the precision-recall curve as a step sum; a random ranking gives the positive rate",
    "calibration": "the property that a model's probabilities match the observed frequencies: 80 % of the compounds given 0.8 are positive",
    "Brier score": "the mean squared difference between the predicted probability and the label",
    "indicator": "the function that is 1 when a statement is true and 0 when it is false",
    "alkyl": "a carbon whose bonds are all single and that is not part of an aromatic ring",
    "N-dealkylation": "removal of a carbon group from a nitrogen by oxidation, the first step of the amine route to time-dependent inhibition",
    "recursive": "in a SMARTS pattern, a description of an atom by a pattern that must match at that atom",
    "lift": "the share of positives among the flagged compounds divided by the share among all compounds",
    "quantitative high-throughput screen": "a screen in which every compound is tested at several concentrations so that a dose-response curve is fitted for each",
    "contingency table": "a table that counts items by two yes-or-no properties at once",
    "odds": "the number of times an event happens divided by the number of times it does not",
    "odds ratio": "the odds in one group divided by the odds in another; 1 when the two groups are alike",
    "null hypothesis": "the assumption that nothing is going on (no difference, no association), against which a result is judged",
    "p-value": "the probability, if the null hypothesis were true, of a result at least as extreme as the one observed",
    "hypergeometric distribution": "the distribution of the number of marked items when a fixed number are drawn at random, without replacement, from a collection",
    "Fisher's exact test": "a test of association in a two-by-two table that sums hypergeometric probabilities",
    "statistically significant": "with a p-value below a chosen limit, conventionally 0.05; it says nothing about the size of the effect",
    "rank test": "a test that uses only the order of the values and not their size",
    "z value": "the distance of a statistic from its expected value, in standard deviations",
    "permutation test": "a test that shuffles group labels many times to see how often chance gives a result as large as the observed one",
    "mmCIF": "the text format in which a structure is written as a table of atoms with their coordinates",
    "pLDDT": "a model's own confidence in the position of an atom, from 0 to 100",
    "Euclidean distance": "the straight-line distance between two points",
    "coordinated": "in a prediction, having a nitrogen of a motif within the coordination distance of the heme iron",
    "van der Waals radius": "the radius of the sphere that represents an atom: the distance at which two non-bonded atoms begin to repel each other",
    "solvent-accessible surface": "the surface traced by the centre of a water-sized probe rolling over the atoms of a molecule",
    "SASA": "the solvent-accessible surface area",
    "buried share": "the share of a molecule's surface that is covered by its surroundings",
    "cavity": "empty space inside a protein that solvent reaches only through a narrow opening or not at all",
    "Streamlit": "a Python library that turns a script into a web page by running it again at every interaction",
    "score on the fitted rows": "the score of a model on the compounds it was fitted on, which rewards memorising",
    "optimism": "the excess of the score on the fitted rows over the score on rows the model has not seen",
    "nested cross-validation": "cross-validation with a second cross-validation inside each training part, so that every choice is made without seeing the rows that score it",
    "outer folds": "the parts held out in turn to score the finished procedure",
    "size effect": "the small gain in score from fitting on more compounds, which an inner model (three quarters of the rows) lacks compared with an outer one",
    "prediction cache": "the stored predictions of every candidate on every task, named after the plan, from which every later analysis is read",
    "Holm's adjustment": "a correction for many tests: the smallest p-value is multiplied by the number of tests, the next by one fewer, and so on",
    "power": "the probability that a test finds a difference that is really there",
    "Tukey's HSD": "a method that compares the means of several groups at once and treats every score as independent",
    "passes the gate": "meets the pre-declared rule: a positive gain with p below 0.05 at both confidence cut-offs",
    "minimum detectable effect": "the smallest true difference that a design finds with a stated probability",
    "replicated": "confirmed by a second, pre-declared design with a smaller variance factor",
    "negative control": "a run on data for which the right answer is nothing (permuted labels), which must give chance",
    "data leakage": "information from the validation rows reaching the fit, which makes a score too high",
    "HOMO": "the highest occupied molecular orbital; its energy says how loosely the most loosely held electrons are bound",
    "LUMO": "the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital, the empty orbital just above the HOMO",
    "dipole moment": "a measure of how unevenly the charge of a molecule is spread, large when one end is positive and the other negative",
    "partial charge": "the share of electronic charge that an atom holds compared with the neutral atom",
    "Fukui index": "the change of the electron density at an atom when an electron is added or removed; it marks the atoms that give up or accept electrons first",
    "semi-empirical method": "an approximate quantum calculation whose parameters are fitted to reference results, about a thousand times faster than an exact one",
    "surrogate model": "a model trained to reproduce the results of a slower calculation",
    "query by committee": "taking the disagreement within a committee of different models as the uncertainty of a prediction",
    "stacked generalisation": "fitting a second model, the meta-learner, to the out-of-fold predictions of several first-level models",
    "meta-learner": "the second-level model of a stack, fitted to the predictions of the first-level models",
    "pool-adjacent-violators algorithm": "the method that fits the monotone step function nearest to a set of ordered labels by merging neighbouring blocks that break the order",
    "expected calibration error": "the weighted average, over bins of predicted probability, of the gap between the mean prediction and the observed share of positives",
    "Platt scaling": "calibrating a score by fitting a logistic function to it",
    "isotonic regression": "calibrating a score by fitting the monotone non-decreasing step function nearest to the labels",
    "SHA-256": "a 64-digit fingerprint of the bytes of a file; any change to the file changes it",
    "structure-activity relationship": "how a change of a molecule's structure changes its activity; the pattern that medicinal chemists read from series of related compounds",
    "matched molecular pair": "two compounds that differ by one small, well-defined structural change, so that the difference of their measured values is the effect of that change",
    "foundation model": "a model pretrained once on a very large collection of data and a general task, whose internal representation is then reused for many specific tasks",
    "importance weighting": "giving each training compound the weight p_test(x) / p_train(x), the ratio of the densities of the test and the training compounds, so that the average loss on the training set estimates the loss on the test set",
    "graph transformer": "an attention network over the atoms of a molecule in which every atom can attend to every other, told where each sits in the graph",
    "joint-embedding predictive architecture": "a network pretrained to predict the learned representation of one part of an input from another part, not to rebuild the input itself",
    "equivariant": "of a network, such that rotating the input rotates the vectors inside the network in the same way",
    "label-free": "of a feature block, computed from the structure alone by models that never saw a challenge label",
    "luciferase": "the enzyme of fireflies that turns its substrate into light; a luminescent assay reads the light as the signal",
    "counter-screen": "a second assay that finds the compounds which disturb the readout itself, so that they can be removed",
    "logD": "the lipophilicity of a compound at a stated pH, here the pH of blood, which allows for ionisation",
    "protein binding": "the share of a compound that sticks to proteins instead of staying free in solution",
    "PXR": "the pregnane X receptor, a protein that senses foreign chemicals and switches on the genes of detoxification enzymes",
    "induction": "raising the amount of an enzyme in the cell, the opposite of inhibiting it",
    "single-concentration screen": "a test of every compound at one concentration, reported as the log2 fold change of the signal",
    "maximal effect": "the plateau that the fitted concentration-response curve reaches: how much of the activity the compound can remove at most",
    "protonation state": "which ionisable groups of a molecule carry a hydrogen ion, and so a charge, at a given pH",
    "softmax": "the function that turns scores into weights, exp(s_k / T) divided by the sum of exp(s_j / T), with a temperature T",
    "wavefunction": "the complex number attached to every configuration of the electrons whose squared size is the probability density of finding them there",
    "Hamiltonian": "the energy operator of a system: the kinetic energy plus the potential energy",
    "eigenfunction": "a function that an operator turns into a multiple of itself; for the Hamiltonian, a state of definite energy",
    "hartree": "the atomic unit of energy, 27.2114 eV or 627.5 kcal/mol",
    "bohr": "the atomic unit of length, 0.529177 angstrom",
    "Pauli principle": "the rule that the wavefunction of electrons changes sign when two of them are exchanged, so that two electrons cannot share an orbital with the same spin",
    "potential energy surface": "the energy of the electrons plus the repulsion of the nuclei as a function of where the nuclei are",
    "geometry optimisation": "moving the nuclei downhill along the forces until the energy is at a minimum",
    "Slater determinant": "the antisymmetric product of one-electron orbitals, written as a determinant",
    "Hartree-Fock": "the approximation in which each electron moves in the average field of the others and the wavefunction is one Slater determinant",
    "self-consistent field": "the iteration in which the orbitals build the field that determines them, repeated until they stop changing",
    "overlap matrix": "the matrix of the integrals of products of pairs of basis functions",
    "density matrix": "the matrix that holds the electron density in a basis, built from the occupied orbital coefficients",
    "two-electron integral": "the repulsion between the charge distribution of one pair of basis functions and that of another pair",
    "Slater-type orbital": "a basis function of the shape exp(-zeta r), the shape of a hydrogen orbital",
    "STO-3G": "the minimal basis in which each Slater-type orbital is replaced by three Gaussians",
    "correlation energy": "the exact energy minus the Hartree-Fock energy, the lowering that comes from electrons avoiding each other",
    "configuration interaction": "the wavefunction written as a mixture of arrangements of the electrons in the orbitals",
    "coupled cluster": "a correlated wavefunction method of high accuracy and a cost that grows steeply with size",
    "Kohn-Sham": "the scheme that replaces interacting electrons by fictitious non-interacting ones with the same density",
    "exchange-correlation energy": "the part of the density functional energy that holds the exchange, the correlation and the correction to the kinetic energy",
    "B3LYP": "a hybrid density functional that mixes 20 percent of exact exchange with approximate exchange and correlation",
    "self-interaction error": "the error of approximate functionals by which an electron partly repels itself",
    "extended Hückel": "a simple model of bonding in which the coupling of two orbitals is proportional to their overlap and the average of their on-site energies",
    "electronic temperature": "the temperature at which the occupations of orbitals near the highest occupied one are smeared",
    "ionisation potential": "the energy needed to remove an electron, here the energy of the cation minus that of the neutral molecule",
    "electron affinity": "the energy gained by adding an electron, here the energy of the neutral molecule minus that of the anion",
    "hardness": "half the difference between the ionisation potential and the electron affinity: the resistance to rearranging electrons",
    "electrophilicity": "the energy lowering of a molecule that takes up electrons, the square of the chemical potential over twice the hardness",
    "ALPB": "the analytical linearised Poisson-Boltzmann model, a continuum description of a solvent",
    "Fukui function": "the change of the electron density when an electron is added or removed, condensed to atoms by finite differences of their charges",
    "proton affinity": "the energy released when a base takes up a proton",
    "Boltzmann weight": "the share of molecules in a shape of energy E at temperature T, proportional to exp(-E / kT)",
    "unified memory": "memory that the processor and the graphics processor of a computer share, so that both draw on the same total",
    "gibibyte": "a unit of memory of 2 to the power 30 (1,073,741,824) bytes, 7 percent more than a gigabyte of a thousand million bytes",
    "broadcasting": "treating an array as if it were repeated along another axis, without storing the repeats",
    "chunking": "cutting the work of a layer into pieces of a few rows, so that only one piece is held in memory at a time",
}
